* sg13cmos5l-bandgap -- closed-loop DC PSRR bench body (issue #30, Part of #5).
* Template: sim/grid-run.sh substitutes @@VDD@@ and @@WORK@@ and writes the deck to
* sim/build/closed-loop-psrr/deck.cir. The model `.lib` cards, the temperature and the
* supply value come from the `klt sim` request (request-grid.json).
*
* This bench's own fixture note: `Vpsrr`, a 0 V DC source in series between the rail source
* and the DUT's vdd net, is the perturbation handle. `corners.supply_v` alters `Vvdd` (the
* grid's supply point); the request's two analysis steps alter `Vpsrr` to -0.05 V and
* +0.05 V, so the DUT's vdd moves by a 0.1 V secant about that point and PSRR @ DC is the
* ratio of the two DC solves. `Vpsrr` must NOT be a `corners.supply_v` key: klt refuses a
* step `alter` of a swept supply source, which is exactly why the handle is a separate
* element rather than a second value of `Vvdd`. A 0 V source is a DC short, so at
* Vpsrr = 0 this deck is electrically the vref bench's deck -- checked at this PDK pin:
* typ/27 C/3.30 V gives the same vref to all six reported digits as the transient bench's
* settled figure. Why `op` and not `tran`, and the resolution/residual guards, are in
* README.md.
*
* The five explanations shared by every grid deck fixture (NO SPICE TITLE LINE,
* rshunt/gmin as convergence aids, the `.global sub!` substrate tie, the `Xdut`
* staged-DUT wrapper, osdi_preload instead of a .control block) are stated once in
* sim/README.md under "Deck fixture conventions", not repeated per template.

.options rshunt=1e9 gmin=1e-9

Vvdd vddsrc 0 dc @@VDD@@
Vpsrr vdd vddsrc dc 0
Vvss vss 0 dc 0
.global sub!
Vsub sub! vss dc 0

.include "@@WORK@@/bandgap_top.spice"
Xdut vdd vref vss sg13cmos5l_bandgap_top
